{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias in rising markets: model underpredicts during strong uptrends (true path sits consistently above forecast, especially days ~1–20). Apply an upward level shift when recent 2–4 week momentum is positive.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: model captures direction but reacts too slowly to rapid rallies (forecast slope is flatter than realized). Add a momentum/acceleration term proportional to recent returns (e.g., last 5–10 days) to steepen the near-term path.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: underprediction is largest in the front half of the horizon (≈days 1–15/20), while late-horizon errors shrink and can flip to slight overprediction when the market turns down. Use a decaying correction (bigger early, smaller later).",
    "Turning-point miss / delayed mean reversion: when the true series peaks and reverses (late in examples), the model stays too high for too long. Introduce a regime switch: if recent volatility is high and price is extended above a moving average, increase mean-reversion strength for days ~15–30.",
    "Oversmoothing of volatility: forecasts are too smooth versus realized swings (misses both upside spikes and subsequent pullbacks). Inflate forecast variance / widen bands and allow larger day-to-day adjustments when recent realized range expands."
  ]
}
